ials and network integrity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"OWE for Securing Public Wi-Fi\",\n      \"explanation\": \"OWE (Opportunistic Wireless Encryption) is a protocol introduced with WPA3 specifically to enhance the security of open, password-free Wi-Fi networks, often found in public spaces. OWE provides automatic, individualized encryption for each device connecting to these networks, preventing passive eavesdropping even without manual authentication [Data: Entities (620, 652, 674), Relationships (1004, 1013, 1041)]. This is a critical improvement over previous standards where open networks offered no encryption, leaving users vulnerable.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Vulnerabilities of WPA2-PSK\",\n      \"explanation\": \"WPA2-PSK, the home mode of WPA2, is susceptible to offline dictionary attacks. This type of attack involves capturing the network's handshake and then using a dictionary of common passwords to crack the pre-shared key without needing to interact with the network in real-time. The security of WPA2-PSK is heavily dependent on the complexity of its password, and weak passwords make it vulnerable to this exploit [Data: Entities (641, 644), Relationships (991, 998)]. WPA3's SAE protocol directly addresses this weakness.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Adoption Requirements for WPA3\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The implementation of WPA3 requires compatible hardware and software. This means that users and organizations may need to update their existing devices, firmware, and operating systems to support the new protocol. While this can present a degree of complexity and potential cost, it is a necessary step to leverage the significantly improved security offered by WPA3 [Data: Entities (620), Relationships (1048)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"WPA3's Role in Modern Security Policies\",\n      \"explanation\": \"WPA3 is recognized as the recommended security protocol for modern Wi-Fi networks. Its advanced feat